**Lesson 2: Object-Oriented Programming in Java - Encapsulation and Inheritance**

**OBJECTIVES:**

1. Explain the concept of encapsulation in Java and how it is achieved using access modifiers.
2. Implement inheritance in Java using the `extends` keyword and understand the role of the `super` keyword.
3. Apply polymorphism using method overriding and overloading in Java.

**TEACHING:**

In the previous lesson, we introduced the core principles of Java, including its object-oriented programming (OOP) paradigm. In this lesson, we will delve deeper into two of the four pillars of OOP in Java: encapsulation and inheritance. Encapsulation is the concept of hiding the implementation details of an object from the outside world and only exposing the necessary information through public methods. This is achieved using access modifiers such as `private`, `protected`, `public`, and default (no modifier).

In Java, access modifiers control the visibility of classes, methods, and fields. The `private` access modifier is the most restrictive, allowing access only within the same class. The `protected` access modifier allows access within the same class and its subclasses. The `public` access modifier allows access from any class, while the default access modifier (no modifier) allows access within the same package. By using these access modifiers, we can encapsulate the data and behavior of an object, making it more secure and easier to maintain.

Inheritance is another fundamental concept in Java OOP. It allows one class to inherit the properties and behavior of another class. The inheriting class is called the subclass or derived class, while the class being inherited from is called the superclass or base class. In Java, inheritance is implemented using the `extends` keyword. The subclass inherits all the fields and methods of the superclass and can also add new fields and methods or override the ones inherited from the superclass. The `super` keyword is used to access the members of the superclass from the subclass.

Polymorphism is the ability of an object to take on multiple forms. In Java, polymorphism is achieved through method overriding and overloading. Method overriding occurs when a subclass provides a different implementation of a method that is already defined in its superclass. Method overloading occurs when multiple methods with the same name can be defined with different parameter lists. This allows objects of different classes to be treated as objects of a common superclass, making it easier to write generic code that can work with a variety of objects.

The Java compiler and runtime environment work together to ensure that the correct method is called when there are multiple methods with the same name. The compiler checks the method signature, including the method name, return type, and parameter list, to determine which method to call. The runtime environment uses dynamic dispatch to resolve the method call at runtime, allowing for more flexibility and polymorphism.

In addition to encapsulation, inheritance, and polymorphism, Java also provides other features that support OOP, such as abstract classes and interfaces. Abstract classes are classes that cannot be instantiated and are intended to be inherited by other classes. Interfaces are abstract classes that define a contract or a set of methods that must be implemented by any class that implements the interface.

**WORKED EXAMPLE:**

Suppose we want to create a simple banking system with different types of accounts, such as checking and savings accounts. We can create an abstract class `Account` that encapsulates the common attributes and behavior of all accounts, such as the account number and balance. We can then create subclasses `CheckingAccount` and `SavingsAccount` that inherit from the `Account` class and add their own specific attributes and behavior.

```java
public abstract class Account {
    private int accountNumber;
    private double balance;

    public Account(int accountNumber, double balance) {
        this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public int getAccountNumber() {
        return accountNumber;
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }

    public abstract void deposit(double amount);
    public abstract void withdraw(double amount);
}

public class CheckingAccount extends Account {
    private double overdraftLimit;

    public CheckingAccount(int accountNumber, double balance, double overdraftLimit) {
        super(accountNumber, balance);
        this.overdraftLimit = overdraftLimit;
    }

    @Override
    public void deposit(double amount) {
        double newBalance = getBalance() + amount;
        setBalance(newBalance);
    }

    @Override
    public void withdraw(double amount) {
        double newBalance = getBalance() - amount;
        if (newBalance < -overdraftLimit) {
            System.out.println("Insufficient funds");
        } else {
            setBalance(newBalance);
        }
    }

    private void setBalance(double balance) {
        // implementation of setting the balance
    }
}

public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    private double interestRate;

    public SavingsAccount(int accountNumber, double balance, double interestRate) {
        super(accountNumber, balance);
        this.interestRate = interestRate;
    }

    @Override
    public void deposit(double amount) {
        double newBalance = getBalance() + amount;
        setBalance(newBalance);
    }

    @Override
    public void withdraw(double amount) {
        double newBalance = getBalance() - amount;
        if (newBalance < 0) {
            System.out.println("Insufficient funds");
        } else {
            setBalance(newBalance);
        }
    }

    private void setBalance(double balance) {
        // implementation of setting the balance
    }
}
```

**COMMON MISTAKE:**

One common mistake that learners make when implementing inheritance in Java is forgetting to use the `super` keyword to access the members of the superclass. This can lead to unexpected behavior and errors, especially when overriding methods. For example, if we override the `deposit` method in the `CheckingAccount` class without calling the `super` method, the balance will not be updated correctly.

