**TEACHING:**

In the previous lessons, we explored the foundations of Java programming, including the language's syntax, data types, and control structures. We also introduced the concept of object-oriented programming (OOP) and its four pillars: encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, and abstraction. In this lesson, we will delve deeper into the principles of OOP in Java, focusing on encapsulation, inheritance, and polymorphism.

Encapsulation is the concept of bundling data and its associated methods that operate on that data within a single unit, called a class. In Java, encapsulation is achieved through the use of access modifiers, such as `private`, `protected`, `public`, and default (also known as package-private). These modifiers control the visibility of class members, ensuring that sensitive data is not exposed to unauthorized access. For example, a `private` field can only be accessed within the same class, while a `public` field can be accessed from any class.

Inheritance is the mechanism by which one class can inherit the properties and behavior of another class. In Java, inheritance is achieved through the use of the `extends` keyword, which allows a subclass to inherit the members of a parent class. The subclass can also add new members or override the members of the parent class. For instance, a `Dog` class can extend an `Animal` class, inheriting its properties and behavior, and then add its own specific characteristics, such as the ability to bark.

Polymorphism is the ability of an object to take on multiple forms, depending on the context in which it is used. In Java, polymorphism is achieved through method overriding and method overloading. Method overriding occurs when a subclass provides a different implementation of a method that is already defined in its parent class. Method overloading occurs when multiple methods with the same name can be defined, but with different parameter lists. For example, a `Shape` class can have multiple subclasses, such as `Circle`, `Rectangle`, and `Triangle`, each of which can override the `area()` method to provide its own implementation.

When designing object-oriented programs in Java, it is essential to consider the trade-offs between composition and inheritance. Composition involves creating objects from other objects, while inheritance involves creating a new class based on an existing class. In general, composition is preferred over inheritance, as it allows for more flexibility and reuse of code. However, inheritance can be useful when there is a clear "is-a" relationship between classes, such as a `Dog` being a type of `Animal`.

In addition to encapsulation, inheritance, and polymorphism, Java provides several other features that support object-oriented programming, such as abstract classes, interfaces, and generics. Abstract classes provide a way to define a class that cannot be instantiated on its own, but can be subclassed by other classes. Interfaces define a contract that must be implemented by any class that implements it. Generics provide a way to define classes, interfaces, and methods that can work with any type of data, while ensuring type safety at compile-time.

